{"id": "e36df2af-0f7f-4dd5-ae57-629a9af5f742", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:27:16.038486", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e0e79446-5ed2-4209-bbed-64e9571e15cd", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Environment confirmed:\n- /workspace contains only task.json (537 bytes). No data, no celebrity dataset, no corpus, no model, no API keys.\n- Python 3.14.4; no numpy/pandas/scipy/torch/transformers/datasets; requests is installed but network is dead (DNS failure) → no API access, no Wikidata downloads, no HF models.\n- CPU only, 224 cores; sqlite available. No GPU.\n- Environment notes: \"Design-only task; repository and compute resources are not required.\"\n\nNow, create the plan (stage=plan) in the researcher's language (Spanish would probably be best — the researcher was writing in Spanish). Content to cover:\n\n1. Inventory: what exists and what doesn't.\n2. Draft approach covering the requested minimum items.\n3. Assumptions and uncertainties.\n\nNo experiments to be run. Hand off with stage=plan.\n\nLet me draft a thorough but scope-limited plan.\n\nHypothesis operationalization:\n- Task A (child→parent): given the child's full name, ask \"Who is [child]'s mother/father?\" — answer = parent name.\n- Task B (parent→child): given the parent's name, ask \"Who is [parent]'s child?\" — answer = child name; ambiguity when there are multiple children.\n- Directional asymmetry hypothesis: accuracy(A) > accuracy(B), even after controls.\n\nCorrect-answer scoring (scoring pipeline):\n- Strict string match is insufficient. Use a 3-tier match: (1) exact-match string normalization (case, diacritics, punctuation, removal of particles like \"van\", \"de\"); (2) alias/artistic-name match via alias table built from ground truth (Wikidata aliases, known pseudonyms); (3) human/secondary verification for borderline cases. For parent→child with multiple children: count as correct if the model outputs one or more correct children? No — pre-register two scoring variants: strict (must list all children; report set-based F1) and lenient (hit@k if at least one correct child is named). Primary metric: set-based P/R/F1 for parent→child; single-slot exact-match/alias accuracy for child→parent. Also record abstention (\"unknown\") separately from incorrect answers.\n\nGround truth:\n- Source: Wikidata (P22 father, P25 mother) — independent from model; cross-verified with a second source (e.g., IMDb family data, or DBpedia/Find a Grave, or human verification via news). Selection rule: pairs present in both sources, or Wikidata plus citation. Key point: ground truth is constructed before querying the model; model disagreement is not allowed to change GT. Also, entity disambiguation via Q-IDs; celebrity criteria: sitelink count / pageview rank.\n- Note on circularity risk: since both the model and GT are partially derived from web text, overlap is expected; the hypothesis under test is about the asymmetry between directions, not about absolute factual accuracy.\n\nSample:\n- Sampling frame: Wikidata entities with occupation/notability filters (e.g., instance of human, with P106 occupation, sitelinks ≥ threshold, pageviews within top N percentile), plus family pairs where both parent and child are independently notable (bidirectional notability requirement — critical control!). Stratification: fame decile, region/language (EN, ES, DE, FR, ZH), era (birth decade), domain (politics, entertainment, sports, royalty).\n- Rationale for requiring both members to be notable: otherwise, parent→child failures may merely reflect that the child is not notable; conversely, child→parent may fail with, e.g., \"raised by grandparents\" cases. But note that requiring both members to be notable biases the sample against the hypothesis (a conservative test), because it excludes the common asymmetry where the famous parent has a non-famous child. Pre-register both: main analysis uses bidirectionally notable pairs; secondary analysis uses a famous-parent → possibly-non-famous-child sample, and discuss the selection effect.\n\nConfounding with co-occurrence frequency (alternative explanation):\n- Corpus co-occurrence (e.g., parent and child names appearing in the same Wikipedia article / Wikipedia2Vec PMI over the dump) can explain both directions; additionally, asymmetric article-link structure: the child's article almost always states \"the son/daughter of X\", whereas the parent's article may not list the children. Measure: (a) whether the parent name appears in the child's Wikipedia article, and vice versa; (b) relative pageviews; (c) relative sitelinks. Include as covariates in the logistic regression / mixed model; matched-pair design: for each pair, the \"mention\" status in both directions — compare the direction where the target is mentioned in the source entity's article versus the direction where it is not mentioned. Test of the directional bias that survives a fair comparison: restrict to pairs where both entities mention each other (mutual mention) and pageviews are within a matched ratio; asymmetry there is a stronger claim about the relational knowledge graph rather than about corpus statistics.\n\nModels:\n- Since there is no network here: the design assumes API or local access will be available later. Pre-register 3–5 models spanning scales/families (e.g., a closed API frontier model, open-weight 70B, open-weight 7–13B, a model from a different lineage), fixed versions/dates, temperature 0, logprobs where available. Two prompt languages (EN + ES), prompt variants (direct QA, fill-in-the-blank, MC with distractors, as an alternative control), order randomization.\n\nDirectional contrasts:\n- Paired comparison per pair: child→parent accuracy minus parent→child accuracy. Primary test: mixed-effects logistic regression with outcome correct ~ direction + log(pageview ratio) + mutual mention + n_children + number of language aliases + family-cluster random intercept. Or, on the matched subset, a McNemar test of direction within pair. Pre-register direction and multiplicity correction (family-wise across models or FDR).\n- Fairness controls: reverse-question format, answer space size (parent→child with k children has a larger answer space; matched condition when n_children=1; MC condition removes the generation/alias confound), popularity matched pairs (children of comparable fame), prompt language, order effects (irrelevant for single-hop but test prompt phrasing).\n\nNull-result reporting:\n- Report effect size with CI (log-odds + accuracy difference), equivalence margin (pre-registered, e.g., |Δacc| < 2pp is a practical null); power analysis for the detectable asymmetry; report per-model, per-stratum; publish all data and scripts; treat \"no asymmetry among mutually-mentioning, fame-matched pairs\" as the most defensible null.\n\nFeasibility in this environment: currently no — no network, no data, no model, no numpy/scipy. Design only; execution requires: snapshot download of Wikidata (or the full JSON subset), Wikipedia dump for co-occurrence (approx. 20GB compressed), or a smaller API-based sampling stage, plus model endpoints. CPU-only precludes local large models; APIs required; budget estimation.\n\nAssumptions:\n- Execution environment will have network/API (not the case here).\n- Wikidata P22/P25 is sufficiently accurate for famous families; errors are non-differential with respect to direction.\n- Celebrity population is Anglocentric unless stratified; pageview rank as a proxy for fame.\n- Sample size ~500–1000 pairs × 2 directions × 2–3 prompt languages × 3–5 models ≈ 12k–60k queries — budget is feasible for a small open model / moderate API cost.\n- No human subjects, no ethical constraints beyond bias reporting.\n\nOpen questions for the researcher:\n- Model list and access (API keys? which families?), compute resource budget, sample size / budget ceiling, whether Wikipedia dump download is permitted, which secondary GT source (IMDb requires a license!), target languages, deadline.\n\nCaveat: IMDb datasets are non-commercial licensed — fine for research but note the licensing terms. Second sources are preferable: Famous Birthdays? Better: cross-check via Wikipedia article text + human spot-check of a random subsample (e.g., 10% double annotation, kappa).\n\nNow I'll write the plan handoff. Language: Spanish (the researcher's). Keep evidence = the findings of my inspection. Required handoff stage: plan. Include assumptions and uncertainties arrays.\n\nKeep the response reasonably concise but complete. The answer field will be the draft plan.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Estado real del entorno (inspección, sin inventar nada)\n\n**Qué HAY en /workspace:** exactamente un archivo, `task.json` (537 bytes), con el enunciado. Nada más.\n\n**Qué NO hay (verificado, no asumido):**\n- Ningún dataset de celebridades, ningún dump de Wikidata/Wikipedia, ningún corpus (busqué `*.jsonl`, `*.ttl`, `*wikidata*`, `*wikipedia*` en el sistema de archivos: cero resultados).\n- Sin red: DNS resuelve a fallo (\"Temporary failure in name resolution\"); no hay acceso a Wikidata, HuggingFace ni APIs de modelos. No hay claves de API en el entorno.\n- Python 3.14.4 con paquetes del sistema; **no** están `numpy`, `pandas`, `scipy`, `torch`, `transformers`, `datasets`, ni clientes de APIs de LLM. `requests` está instalado pero es inútil sin red. `sqlite3` sí funciona.\n- 224 cores CPU, sin GPU → inviable correr modelos locales grandes aquí.\n- El propio task.json dice: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\nConclusión honesta: **este entorno solo permite diseño**. La ejecución del experimento requiere un entorno con red (descarga de Wikidata/Wikipedia o SPARQL) y acceso a modelos (API o pesos locales en otra máquina). No afirmo nada \"probado\".\n\n## 2) Borrador de enfoque (acotado)\n\n**A. Operacionalización de la direccionalidad.** Dos tareas de un salto sobre los mismos pares (parent P, child C):\n- D1 (hijo→padre/madre): \"¿Quién es el padre/la madre de C?\" — respuesta de un solo hueco.\n- D2 (padre→hijo): \"¿Quién es el/los hijo(s) de P?\" — respuesta de conjunto (k hijos posibles).\n- Hipótesis: acc(D1) > acc(D2) de forma robusta tras controles.\n\n**Acierto (pre-registrado, 3 niveles):** (1) match exacto tras normalización (caja, diacíticos, partículas \"de/van\", comillas); (2) match de alias vía tabla de alias construida SOLO con el ground truth (Wikidata `skilled by`/aliases, nombres artísticos); (3) revisión humana de casos frontera. Para D2: métrica primaria F1 de conjunto (deben nombrarse todos los hijos notables del par); métrica secundaria hit (≥1 hijo correcto), reportando ambas. Abstenciones (\"no lo sé\") se codifican aparte de respuestas incorrectas.\n\n**B. Ground truth y anti-circularidad.** Fuente primaria: Wikidata (P22 padre, P25 madre), identificadores Q-ID para desambiguar. Criterio de inclusión: corroboración en una segunda fuente independiente (texto del artículo de Wikipedia del propio hijo/menciones cruzadas) + auditoría humana del 10% con doble anotación (informar kappa). **Regla dura:** el GT se congela ANTES de consultar cualquier modelo, y el desacuerdo del modelo nunca modifica el GT. Admitimos la circularidad residual (modelo y GT se nutren de la misma web); por eso la hipótesis se formula como *asimetría entre direcciones*, no como veracidad absoluta.\n\n**C. Muestra.** Marco: humanos de Wikidata con dos veces notables (padre e hijo con sitelinks ≥ umbral y pageviews en percentil alto), estratificada por decil de fama, dominio (política, entretenimiento, deporte, realeza), idioma/región (EN, ES, DE, FR, ZH) y década de nacimiento del hijo. Motivo de \"doble notabilidad\": sin ella, fallar D2 puede ser solo \"el hijo no es notable\" (artefacto de memoria, no sesgo relacional). Pre-registrar un análisis secundario con padres famosos → hijos potencialmente no notables, discutiéndolo como prueba NO conservadora. Frecuencia de co-ocurrencia como explicación alternativa se mide, no se ignora: (i) ¿menciona el artículo de C a P y viceversa? (ii) PMI/co-ocurrencia en dump de Wikipedia; (iii) ratio de pageviews P/C. Estas entran como covariables y como criterio de emparejamiento (ver D).\n\n**D. Contraste direccional justo.** Comparación pareada por familia. Condición clave para que sea justa: **subconjunto de mención recíproca y fama emparejada** (ambos artículos se mencionan mutuamente; pageviews dentro de un ratio fijo; preferir k=1 hijo para igualar tamaño del espacio de respuesta). Prueba principal sobre ese subconjunto; el resto del set alimenta el análisis de covariables. Controles: formato de pregunta invertido, prompts EN+ES, 3 variantes de prompt (QA libre, cloze, opción múltiple con distractores de la misma familia — la versión MC elimina el confuso de generación/alias), orden de opciones aleatorizado, temperatura 0, versión de modelo fijada. Modelos pre-registrados: 3–5 de al menos 3 linajes y 2 escalas (p. ej., un frontier API, un open-weights ~70B, uno ~8B), con el mismo presupuesto de tokens.\n\n**E. Análisis.** Modelo mixto: correcto ~ dirección + log-ratio pageviews + mención recíproca + n_hijos + idioma_prompt + (1|familia) + fija (modelo). Test no paramétrico complementario: McNemar por par dentro de la condición emparejada, por modelo, con corrección FDR. Tamaño de efecto reportado como diferencia de precisión con IC y odds ratio. **Resultado nulo:** pre-registrar margen de equivalencia (|Δacc| ≤ 2 pp = nulo práctico), power analysis previa (detectar Δ=5 pp con 80% de poder con ~N pares, calcular con piloto de simulación de poder, no con datos reales), y tabla por modelo/estrato. Un nulo en la condición emparejada se reporta como \"no hay asimetría más allá de estadísticas de corpus\", que es el hallazgo más defendible.\n\n## 3) Supuestos e incertidumbres (explícitos)\n\n**Supuestos:** (a) la ejecución ocurrirá en otro entorno con red y