C control on a random 400-pair subset × 2 directions × 1 language (EN) × 4 models = 3.2k; total about 22k calls. Cost assumption: about 600 prompt tokens + 100 completion tokens per call → about 700 tokens average → 15.4M input? Calculation: prompt about 500 tokens, completion about 80 tokens. Total input about 22k×500=11M tokens; output 22k×80=1.76M tokens. Cost at mixed pricing: frontier API about $3/M input, $15/M output → $33 + $26 ≈ $59? Actual typical frontier pricing is $3/M input, $15/M output. 11M/1M·$3=$33; 1.76M·$15=$26.4 → about $60 per frontier model. Open weights are much cheaper or local. State that prices are placeholders/assumptions and note the range. Time: with rate limit 100 RPM, 22k calls ≈ 3.7 hours; if serialized per model then about 15 hours wall-clock total; conservatively state 1-2 days.\n\nIf budget is halved: halving of models (4→2, keep frontier+open), reduce languages (2→1, main is EN, ES kept as a control only on subset), pool 1200→800; explicitly state what gets dropped: drop ZH/DE/FR strata or reduce, drop MC control subset? Better: drop secondary language on the full set, reduce secondary analysis (notable children) — keep the primary matched condition as is.\n\nDecision rule (point 6):\n- SUPPORTED: on primary condition, McNemar p<0.05/… AND mixed model direction OR of <1 with CI entirely on the biased side (i.e., D1 acc − D2 acc point estimate ≥ +2pp AND one-sided 95% CI lower bound > 0), AND observed direction consistent across ≥4/5 models, AND asymmetry persists when controlling for mutual mention (by construction). Formalized: supported when across all pre-registered models Δacc (D1−D2) is positive on primary subset AND FDR-corrected McNemar p<0.05 for the aggregated model AND equivalence test rejects |Δ|≤2pp in the positive direction.\n- REFUTED: if TOST equivalence rejects |Δacc|>2pp (i.e., CI within ±2pp) with adequate power, OR if Δacc is significantly negative (reverse-direction bias) — in the latter case, refutation + note reverse-direction finding.\n- UNDETERMINED: if CI spans both the null region and substantial effects; underpowering (discordance-based power <80%); or if model disagreement exceeds pre-registered bounds (mixed direction across models with no fixed-effect pattern).\n\nMC distractor matching (point 3): Distractors are same relationship type, matched on gender (if asking about mother), birth year ±5 years, nationality/country of residence, domain, ±0.5 log pageviews. Expected chance = 1/num_options (k=4 → 25%) reported; use as sanity floor; MC accuracy is corrected via chance-normalization or reported raw with chance overlay.\n\nParent vs. mother as factor (point 4): relationship_type ∈ {father, mother} as fixed effect and interaction direction × relationship_type; pre-registered directional prediction that asymmetry is larger for the maternal line (mothers are less salient in the corpus → parent→mother... wait. Direction: D1 asks for parent given child. The asymmetry of hypothesis D1 vs. D2 can be stronger for mothers because maternal lineage is under-reported; also test separately father pairs and mother pairs; report stratified Δ.\n\nSection on exposure vs. relational bias (point 2): Dedicated \"Separation of Exposure and Relational Bias\" section: the primary condition by design equalizes exposure (mutual mention within article, pageviews within ratio). Additionally covariate adjustment and an \"exposure gradient\" analysis: within matched subsets, further split on mention-salience (target named in first paragraph vs. later section; frequency of named occurrences) — if asymmetry tracks gradient → exposure artifact; if asymmetry persists within gradient bins → relational claim. Refutation scenario: if a strong Δ appears only in unmatched/mutual-mention-lacking sets and is absent within mutual-matching pairs, classify as corpus exposure artifact, not directional relational bias.\n\nAlso clarify anchoring: primary test = paired accuracy difference after difficulty matching, not raw accuracy difference; raw Δ is reported as descriptive.\n\nAlso explicitly state the selection and justification of the k trade-off.\n\nPower: compute exactly in python. Also compute cost table with simple arithmetic. Execute several checks (design-stage calculation, not experiment). Then submit result.\n\nSample size for TOST equivalence margin 2pp: to claim equivalence, CI width <... need n such that CI half-width at Δ̂=0 is ≤2pp: roughly se ≈ sqrt((p10+p01)/n)?? For paired accuracy difference the variance depends on discordance. Half-width ≈ 1.96·sqrt((p10+p01−(p10−p01)²)/n) ≤ 0.02. With p10=0.08, p01=0.06: var term=0.14−0.0004=0.1396; required n=(1.96²·0.1396)/0.0004 = 3.8416·0.1396/0.0004 = 0.5363/0.0004=1341. About 1340 pairs for a tight equivalence claim. Good — state: with 600 pairs, equivalence margin is limited to about ±3pp; can compute margin for 600: half-width=1.96·sqrt(0.1396/600)=1.96·0.01525=0.0299 → about 3pp. So equivalence margin is data-dependent; adjust pre-registered margin as a function of observed discordance? No — margin is fixed at 2pp, if n is insufficient the result will be \"undetermined\"; or pre-register equivalence margin 3pp if n<1000. Let's decide: margin fixed at 2pp; minimum n for that at anticipated discordance about 1350; if funnel produces <1000 pairs in primary subset, margin is pre-registered as max(2pp, achievable half-width at anticipated discordance 0.14) — hmm, that's data-dependent. Cleaner: pre-register margin 3pp (achievable with about 600 pairs), noting it's less stringent. Actually 2pp has better scientific defensibility. Compromise: if primary n≥1200, margin δ=2pp, else δ=3pp, both pre-specified with their respective required n — this is acceptable pre-specification (function of n, not function of observed effect).\n\nLet's compute the numbers in python and put them in the table.\n\nFunnel: state clearly as planning assumption: seed pool 1500 bidirectional pairs (over-sampled because some families will fail checks); filters: k=1 notable child (about 40%?), mutual mention (about 70%), fame ratio ≤3 (about 60%) → about 1500·0.4·0.7·0.6 ≈ 252?? Too low. Better to build the funnel with oversampling: seed candidates from families where child has a \"child\" statement; k=1 notable children is probably common among e.g. political dynasties. Present target and expansion rule explicitly: start by collecting candidates until primary subset reaches N=600 (quota sampling rather than estimated survival rate), cap 4000 seeds. Quota sampling risk: selection bias — mitigation: random selection from candidate list until quota met, report funnel rates, stratification by quotas.\n\nAlso state clearly that in the sandbox we can run nothing.\n\nNumber of models: pre-register 5: Frontier A (API), Frontier B (API), open 70B, open 8-13B, different-lineage mid-size. Calls: primary 1200... wait, if quota 600 in primary subset and full pool 1200. Main run on full pool of 1200 pairs (both directions, 2 languages)? For 5 models → 1200·2·2·5 = 24k calls. MC control: random 400 pairs from primary subset × 2 directions × EN × 5 models = 4k. Total about 28k. Slightly exceeds 20k; adjust: main on primary subset only? No — the full set is needed for covariate/gradient analyses. Keep about 28k; state range. Halve: drop 1 API model and 1 open, languages EN only → 1200·2·1·3=7.2k + MC 2.4k ≈ 9.6k. OK.\n\nLet's quickly compute the power calculation.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport math\nza, zb = 1.959964, 0.8416212\ndef mcnemar_n(p10,p01):\n    p=p10+p00_=p01; s=p10+p01; d=(p10-p01)\n    return ((za*math.sqrt(s)+zb*math.sqrt(s-d*d))**2)/(d*d)\nfor p10,p01 in [(0.15,0.05),(0.12,0.07),(0.10,0.05),(0.08,0.06)]:\n    print(f\"p10={p10} p01={p01} delta={abs(p10-p01):.2f} -> n={math.ceil(mcnemar_n(p10,p01))}\")\n# TOST half-width given disc rates and n\nfor n in [600,900,1200,1350]:\n    s=0.14; d=0.0\n    hw=za*math.sqrt((s-d*d)/n)\n    print(f\"n={n} disc_sum=0.14 -> 95%CI half-width={hw*100:.2f} pp\")\n# cost model\ncalls = 1200*2*2*5 + 400*2*1*5\nprint(\"total calls:\", calls)\ntok_in = calls*550; tok_out=calls*90\nprint(f\"tokens: in={tok_in/1e6:.1f}M out={tok_out/1e6:.2f}M\")\nfor pin,pout in [(3,15),(0.6,2.5)]:\n    print(f\"price {pin}/{pout} USD/M -> ${tok_in/1e6*pin + tok_out/1e6*pout:.0f}\")\nprint(\"hours at 60 rpm:\", calls/60, \"at 600 rpm:\", calls/600)\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8de369606b52520d", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8de369606b52520d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\nimport math\\nza, zb = 1.959964, 0.8416212\\ndef mcnemar_n(p10,p01):\\n    p=p10+p00_=p01; s=p10+p01; d=(p10-p01)\\n    return ((za*math.sqrt(s)+zb*math.sqrt(s-d*d))**2)/(d*d)\\nfor p10,p01 in [(0.15,0.05),(0.12,0.07),(0.10,0.05),(0.08,0.06)]:\\n    print(f\\\"p10={p10} p01={p01} delta={abs(p10-p01):.2f} -> n={math.ceil(mcnemar_n(p10,p01))}\\\")\\n# TOST half-width given disc rates and n\\nfor n in [600,900,1200,1350]:\\n    s=0.14; d=0.0\\n    hw=za*math.sqrt((s-d*d)/n)\\n    print(f\\\"n={n} disc_sum=0.14 -> 95%CI half-width={hw*100:.2f} pp\\\")\\n# cost model\\ncalls = 1200*2*2*5 + 400*2*1*5\\nprint(\\\"total calls:\\\", calls)\\ntok_in = calls*550; tok_out=calls*90\\nprint(f\\\"tokens: in={tok_in/1e6:.1f}M out={tok_out/1e6:.2f}M\\\")\\nfor pin,pout in [(3,15),(0.6,2.5)]:\\n    print(f\\\"price {pin}/{pout} USD/M -> ${tok_in/1e6*pin + tok_out/1e6*pout:.0f}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"hours at 60 rpm:\\\", calls/60, \\\"at 600 rpm:\\\", calls/600)\\nEOF\", \"summary\": \"Compute McNemar power, equivalence widths, cost table\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-62b5021e-168e-48e5-b5d2-f80f77ce5d8d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Compute McNemar power, equivalence widths, cost table", "kind